ets give Bell only about a 4 percent chance.", "watch": "Only a major late scandal or a sharply Democratic national tide would put this in play, and the Libertarian on the ballot could shave a point or two without threatening the outcome.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Pat Harrigan (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Pat Harrigan enters the fall as a heavy favorite in a North Carolina district that leans Republican by about nine points. Every major race rating places it in Safe or Solid Republican territory, with Decision Desk HQ the most cautious at Likely Republican. Prediction markets give Democrat Ashley Bell roughly a 4 percent chance of an upset.\n\nThe money gap is stark: Harrigan has raised about $1.08 million with $451,000 on hand, while Bell has raised $84,000 with $26,000 available. The only public poll, a March survey from a Republican-sponsored firm, showed Harrigan ahead 52-37 among likely voters. Harrigan has spent the summer touting legislation on health access, the electric grid, and foreign access to national labs, and no recent developments suggest the race is competitive.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0414, "margin": -11.04}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0292, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0146, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0158, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0277, "margin": -13.0}]}